<p class="asof">Vendor features change monthly. Everything here was read from public docs, launch posts and source code on 8 October 2026; check the linked sources for current behaviour.</p>

<main class="wrap">

<section id="what" style="padding-top:36px">
  <p class="intro" style="color:var(--ink)">Tell an assistant you are in Singapore, and weeks after you fly home its memory may still say you are there. The example is OpenAI's, and so is the fix: a process that rewrites memory in the background as time passes, so "You're going to Singapore in July" becomes "You went to Singapore in July 2026". OpenAI calls the process dreaming.</p>
  <p class="intro" style="color:var(--ink)">ChatGPT is not the only one. Anthropic, Devin, Honcho, OpenClaw and Letta also run a second memory writer while you are away, and it can merge, replace, delete and infer. The vendors present it as an improvement. None has released a test of dreaming itself that someone else could rerun.</p>
  <h2>What is dreaming in AI memory?</h2>
  <p class="intro" style="color:var(--ink)"><b>Dreaming</b> is memory writing that runs outside the conversation. A model reads stored memory together with many past sessions, then merges duplicates, replaces outdated entries, deletes notes, and adds inferences that no single session stated. It runs on a schedule, after a threshold, or continually in the background. Normal memory writing runs inside the conversation and mostly records what was said.</p>
</section>

<!-- ================= I ================= -->
<section id="i">
  <div class="numeral">I</div>
  <h2>Day and night</h2>
  <p class="intro">Every memory system has a writer that runs during the conversation. Dreaming adds a second writer that runs outside it. The second writer reads many sessions together and edits memory across them, which the first writer cannot do.</p>
  <table class="diptych" style="margin-top:22px">
    <caption class="vh">Normal memory writing (the day writer) compared with dreaming (the night writer)</caption>
    <colgroup><col class="lab"><col><col></colgroup>
    <thead>
      <tr><td class="lab"></td><th scope="col" class="day"><span class="caps">The day writer</span><span class="sub">normal memory writing, inside a session</span></th><th scope="col" class="night"><span class="caps">The night writer</span><span class="sub">dreaming, outside the conversation: on a schedule, after a threshold, or continually</span></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">SEES</th><td class="day">one session or one batch of messages</td><td class="night">stored memory plus many sessions (Anthropic: 1 to 100)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">READS ITS OWN PAST OUTPUT</th><td class="day">rarely; it mostly appends</td><td class="night">always; the store is its input</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">DELETES OR REPLACES</th><td class="day">rarely</td><td class="night">yes; five of the six systems here document it</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">WRITES</th><td class="day">what was said. Honcho's writer prompt: facts "derived directly from their messages"</td><td class="night">also things nobody said: deductions, patterns, "insights", "lessons missed"</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">BUDGET</th><td class="day">one fast call per batch (Honcho) or a few file edits (Anthropic, Devin)</td><td class="night">a multi-step agent, often a larger model</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">WHO IS WATCHING</th><td class="day">the user; the next turn can catch a mistake</td><td class="night">no one at write time; at most, the user sees the result later</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

