<!-- ================= V ================= -->
<section id="v">
  <div class="numeral">V</div>
  <h2>Where dreams go wrong</h2>
  <p class="intro">Each failure below has either been measured in a paper (gold) or is allowed by a product's design and has not been measured (terracotta). The papers show what consolidation can do in a research setup, not how often it happens inside any product on this page.</p>
  <div class="risks" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="marble risk-card"><div class="status measured">MEASURED</div><h3 class="caps">Rewrites compound</h3><p>Memory updated again and again by an LLM first helps, then degrades, sometimes below having no memory; keeping raw episodes was competitive. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12978" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Zhang et al., arXiv 2605.12978</em></a>.</p></div>
    <div class="marble risk-card"><div class="status measured">MEASURED</div><h3 class="caps">The tense is lost</h3><p>In 244 of 381 matched pairs, a memory writer turned "I am driving a Peugeot" into "drives a Peugeot" while keeping "I drive a Peugeot" as said, and never the reverse. The pattern held in all 11 configurations and in mem0, Graphiti and Letta. <em><a href="/blog/aspect-persistence-eternal-present-memory/">LAPSE</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36457" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv 2609.36457</a></em>. ChatGPT's dreamer is built to make the opposite move, from "going to" to "went", and <a href="https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-memory-dreaming/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI reports</a> a gain on its own time evaluation.</p></div>
    <div class="marble risk-card"><div class="status measured">MEASURED</div><h3 class="caps">The speaker's standing is lost</h3><p>Consolidation kept a claim but dropped the limits on who said it and how it may be used in 48 of 49 configurations. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01679" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>AuthMem-Bench, arXiv 2608.01679</em></a>.</p></div>
    <div class="marble risk-card"><div class="status open">ALLOWED, NOT MEASURED</div><h3 class="caps">Traits from two facts</h3><p><a href="https://github.com/plastic-labs/honcho/blob/main/src/dreamer/specialists.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Honcho's induction</a> may write a personality trait from two observations. Whether such traits are stable across runs or predict what the person says next is unknown.</p></div>
    <div class="marble risk-card"><div class="status open">ALLOWED, NOT MEASURED</div><h3 class="caps">Forgetting the rare</h3><p><a href="https://docs.devin.ai/product-guides/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Devin deletes notes</a> no session has used. A fact needed once a year is, by that rule, a candidate for deletion.</p></div>
    <div class="marble risk-card"><div class="status open">ALLOWED, NOT MEASURED</div><h3 class="caps">No one awake</h3><p>In the in-place designs (<a href="https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-memory-dreaming/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ChatGPT</a>, <a href="https://github.com/plastic-labs/honcho" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Honcho</a>, <a href="https://docs.devin.ai/product-guides/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Devin</a>, <a href="https://docs.letta.com/guides/agents/architectures/sleeptime" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Letta</a>, <a href="https://docs.openclaw.ai/concepts/dreaming" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenClaw</a>) a bad night reaches the next session unless someone reads what changed. ChatGPT's summary, by <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148-memory-faq" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI's own description</a>, may leave out details that memory can still use.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="rule"></div>

<!-- ================= VI ================= -->
<section id="vi">
  <div class="numeral">VI</div>
  <h2>Three ways to study a dream</h2>
  <div class="lenses" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="marble lens">
      <svg class="medallion" viewBox="0 0 92 92" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="46" cy="46" r="42" fill="none" stroke="#a8843f" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="46" cy="46" r="36" fill="none" stroke="#a8843f" stroke-dasharray="2 4"/><g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="46" y1="24" x2="46" y2="66"/><line x1="26" y1="34" x2="66" y2="34"/></g><path d="M20 50 L26 34 L32 50 Z M60 50 L66 34 L72 50 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/></svg>
      <h3 class="caps">Psychometrics</h3>
      <p>Treat a dream as a measurement. <em>Reliability:</em> run the same night twice; do the same traits and inferences come back? <em>Validity:</em> do the inferred traits predict what the person says later? Honcho's trait inference makes this literal.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="marble lens">
      <svg class="medallion" viewBox="0 0 92 92" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="46" cy="46" r="42" fill="none" stroke="#a8843f" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M28 56 C 18 40, 30 22, 48 24 C 66 26, 70 44, 60 52 C 54 58, 56 66, 52 70 L40 70 C 40 64, 34 62, 28 56 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M40 36 C 46 32, 52 36, 50 42 M46 46 C 52 44, 58 48, 54 54" fill="none" stroke="#a8843f"/></svg>
      <h3 class="caps">Cognition</h3>
      <p>Human sleep consolidation has known errors: the gist kept while details go, confusion about who said what, related items remembered that were never seen. Each predicts a specific failure in a dreamer. Write the predictions down before running anything.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="marble lens">
      <svg class="medallion" viewBox="0 0 92 92" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="46" cy="46" r="42" fill="none" stroke="#a8843f" stroke-width="2"/><polygon points="22,40 46,24 70,40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="30" y1="42" x2="30" y2="64"/><line x1="46" y1="42" x2="46" y2="64"/><line x1="62" y1="42" x2="62" y2="64"/><line x1="22" y1="66" x2="70" y2="66"/></g></svg>
      <h3 class="caps">Real use</h3>
      <p>Honcho and OpenClaw are open source and keep enough history to diff a store before and after a night. Anthropic keeps both stores and the dream's transcript, behind preview access. Devin shows each night's changes, but memory is private. ChatGPT shows a summary rather than the memory itself, so a night's changes are hard to see from outside.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="rule"></div>

<!-- ================= VII ================= -->
<section id="vii" class="faq">
  <div class="numeral">VII</div>
  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <h3>What is AI dreaming?</h3>
  <p>Dreaming is memory maintenance that runs outside the conversation. A separate model run reviews past sessions and stored memory, then rewrites or extends that memory so later sessions start from a cleaner, more complete version. The name borrows from sleep, when people consolidate memories.</p>
  <h3>How is dreaming different from normal memory writing?</h3>
  <p>Normal writing runs inside a conversation, sees one session, and mostly appends. Dreaming runs outside the conversation, sees many sessions and stored memory at once, reads its own earlier output, deletes and replaces entries, and writes inferences nobody said. No user is present to catch a mistake.</p>
  <h3>Which AI products use dreaming?</h3>
  <p>As of 8 October 2026: ChatGPT (since April 2025, rebuilt in June 2026), Anthropic's Claude Managed Agents (research preview), Devin by Cognition (about once a day), Honcho by Plastic Labs (open source, since October 2025), OpenClaw (nightly), and Letta (after a set number of steps or on context compaction).</p>
  <h3>Does ChatGPT have dreaming?</h3>
  <p>Yes. OpenAI <a href="https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-memory-dreaming/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">added a first version</a> in April 2025, which curated memory from chat history in the background alongside saved memories. In June 2026 it made a rebuilt version, Dreaming V3, the foundation of ChatGPT memory. It updates memories as time passes and shows the result on a memory summary page, where you can correct an entry or tell ChatGPT not to mention it again. Temporary chats do not create or update memories.</p>
  <h3>Is dreaming the same as sleep-time compute?</h3>
  <p>They are related. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13171" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sleep-time compute</a> (Lin et al., 2025) has a model reason about a context before questions arrive. Dreaming applies offline work to the memory store itself. OpenClaw's docs name sleep-time compute as an inspiration, and Letta now calls its sleep-time agents "dreaming".</p>
  <h3>Does dreaming make memory better?</h3>
  <p>OpenAI and Anthropic report that it does; neither has released a test that others can rerun. OpenAI compares its 2024, 2025 and 2026 memory systems on its own evaluations of recall, preferences and time awareness, and reports gains on each without publishing the evaluations. Anthropic's launch post reports one customer's completion rates rising about 6x "in their tests", without the task set or baseline. An <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12978" target="_blank" rel="noopener">independent study</a> found that memory rewritten again and again by an LLM can end up worse than no memory.</p>
  <h3>Can users see or correct what a dream wrote?</h3>
  <p>It depends on the product. ChatGPT shows a memory summary that you can correct or delete, though <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148-memory-faq" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI says</a> it may not include every detail memory can use. Anthropic writes each dream to a new store, and a person decides whether to use it. Devin lists every dream and what it changed. OpenClaw keeps a copy of the file it rewrote and a diary of each night. Honcho and Letta leave review to the developer; Letta can have a second agent check the changes first.</p>
</section>

